{% set local_problem %}
<p>Let me show you what lake humidity does to sectional gutter seams.</p>

<p>Every 10 feet, sectional gutters have a joint where two pieces connect. That joint is sealed with caulk or a rubber gasket when it's installed. In a dry climate, those seals hold up for 15-20 years. Near Lake Guntersville? More like 8-10.</p>

<p>The problem is moisture cycling. The seal absorbs a little humidity overnight, dries out during the day, absorbs more the next night. Each cycle weakens the material slightly. After a few thousand cycles, the seal cracks. Water gets in. And once water penetrates a seam, it accelerates the failure — corroding the aluminum edges, loosening the connection, letting in more water.</p>

<p>I pulled sectional gutters off a house at <a href="/recent-projects/">Goose Pond</a> last year that were only 12 years old. Every seam was either leaking or about to. The homeowner thought his gutters were fine because they weren't visibly falling apart. But the fascia behind those seams? Rotted through. $2,400 in wood repair before we could hang new gutters.</p>

<p>Seamless gutters eliminate most of those failure points. No mid-run seams means no mid-run leaks. The only joints are at corners and downspout connections — and those we seal with proper gutter sealant (not silicone, which lets go eventually), reinforced with rivets.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are exactly what they sound like: continuous aluminum channels with no splices along their length.</p>

<p>We fabricate them on-site using a portable roll-forming machine in our truck. Aluminum coil stock goes in one end, finished gutter comes out the other — cut to whatever length your home needs. A 47-foot run gets a 47-foot gutter. Not five 10-foot sections joined together with seams that'll eventually leak.</p>

<p>The machine forms the gutter profile as it extrudes — we run 5-inch and 6-inch K-style (the standard residential profile with the decorative front), plus half-round for historic homes that need it. Color is baked into the aluminum finish, not painted on, so it won't peel or flake.</p>

<p>For Scottsboro lake properties, we default to 0.032" gauge aluminum instead of the standard 0.027". That extra thickness adds corrosion resistance and dent resistance — both of which matter more in the humid lake environment. The cost difference is about $75-100 on a typical house. Worth it.</p>

<p>Installation means removing old gutters (if present), inspecting and repairing fascia, hanging the new gutters with proper slope (1/4" per 10 feet toward downspouts), and sealing all joints with professional-grade gutter sealant. We test drainage with a hose before we leave.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been installing seamless gutters in Jackson County for over 20 years. We understand what Lake Guntersville humidity does to homes, and we spec materials and techniques accordingly.</p>

<p>Our standard for lake properties is heavier gauge aluminum, closer hanger spacing (18" instead of 24"), and factory-sealed joints at corners. It costs slightly more but lasts significantly longer in this environment.</p>

<p>For <a href="/recent-projects/">vacation homeowners</a>: we can coordinate around your schedule, provide access-while-away installation, and document everything with photos. You'll know exactly what was done even if you weren't there to see it.</p>
